1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Rays that make the enemy flee when they feel the heat.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
And devastating lasers straight out of science fiction.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
A machine gun for BBs, super slippery slime,

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and a flashing light that can make you lose your lunch.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
From ancient devices designed to rip, tear, and ensnare,

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
to robo-warriors of the future,

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
it's the untold story of Strange Weapons on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Today's modern arsenal is made up of more than just bullets, blades, and bombs.

9
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:58,000
Weapons experts are working overtime, inventing ingenious new ways to kill, subdue, or repel.

10
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:01,840
And their unusual creations are quickly changing

11
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:05,340
the face of warfare and law enforcement.

12
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:13,520
These service personnel playing the role of an unruly mob

13
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:15,920
at Georgia's Moody Air Force Base

14
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,560
are about to fall prey to an invisible ray.

15
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:21,260
The hulking panel atop this Humvee

16
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,420
is part of what the US military calls

17
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,600
the active denial system, or ADS.

18
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:30,000
It's designed to incapacitate enemy combatants

19
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,040
with an unnerving non-lethal sensation of intense heat.

20
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,440
Watch as the ray silently strikes and scatters the crowd.

21
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:45,780
The active denial system has three great characteristics.

22
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:47,720
First of all, it's safe.

23
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:49,460
Second, it's effective.

24
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,560
And third, it has a tremendous range

25
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:53,620
compared to the other non-lethal weapons

26
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:55,500
that today's warfighter has.

27
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,700
This is the heart of this 100 kilowatt transmitter.

28
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,020
This is the gyrotron.

29
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:04,440
200 kilowatts of electricity is fed in,

30
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:07,160
and 100 kilowatts of radio frequency comes out.

31
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:11,520
That millimeter wave energy comes out an aperture

32
00:02:11,521 --> 00:02:13,260
underneath the main reflector,

33
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:16,780
hits the subreflector, which illuminates that main reflector,

34
00:02:16,781 --> 00:02:20,480
and sends a roughly antenna-sized beam downrange.

35
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,880
Those holes that you see in the antenna

36
00:02:24,881 --> 00:02:28,340
or for the cameras and other visual equipment that the operator used

37
00:02:28,341 --> 00:02:30,380
so that he knows exactly where that beam is going.

38
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,460
It's operated by a joystick.

39
00:02:32,461 --> 00:02:35,020
The operator looks into the console,

40
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,240
sees exactly what that antenna is aimed at,

41
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:41,920
moves the joystick left, antenna slews to the left,

42
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:43,100
same way to the right.

43
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:46,600
Then when there's an individual who's identified as a troublemaker,

44
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:50,120
he has a cursor, he can put that cursor on that individual,

45
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,180
pull the trigger that's on the joystick,

46
00:02:52,181 --> 00:02:55,180
and the energy is sent downrange at the speed of light.

47
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:58,900
The electromagnetic radiation released by the active denial system

48
00:02:58,901 --> 00:03:01,620
is similar to the microwaves in your microwave oven

49
00:03:01,621 --> 00:03:04,420
in that it causes the water molecules in the target

50
00:03:04,421 --> 00:03:06,320
to become excited and heat up.

51
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,800
But that's where the similarity ends.

52
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:13,000
The ADS is designed to heat only the very surface of the skin.

53
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:16,420
It does this by outputting only the carefully chosen

54
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:19,240
radio wave frequency of 95 gigahertz.

55
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:25,240
Even though it can easily penetrate clothing, the ADS generates a much shorter and safer

56
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:29,240
wavelength of radio waves than those used in microwave ovens.

57
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:34,240
The active denial system millimeter wave directed energy beam reaches 1 64th of an inch into

58
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:35,240
human skin.

59
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:39,240
So that is the most outermost layer of the skin, roughly equivalent to about three sheets

60
00:03:39,241 --> 00:03:41,240
of notebook paper.

61
00:03:41,241 --> 00:03:46,240
It is essentially affecting the pain nerves in the outermost layer of the skin, heating

62
00:03:46,241 --> 00:03:48,240
them up and causing an immediate repel effect.

63
00:03:48,241 --> 00:03:50,240
Even these stoic servicemen,

64
00:03:50,241 --> 00:03:52,240
aware of what's about to happen,

65
00:03:52,241 --> 00:03:56,240
can't help but flinch when they feel the heat.

66
00:03:56,241 --> 00:03:58,240
This is the first time I've experienced the beam

67
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:01,240
from the active denial system, and it feels like an intense

68
00:04:01,241 --> 00:04:04,240
warmth feeling, kind of similar to opening a very hot oven door.

69
00:04:04,241 --> 00:04:07,240
And it's a compelling feeling that you want to get out of the way

70
00:04:07,241 --> 00:04:08,240
of this beam.

71
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:11,240
If you were not expecting this, it would very definitely shock you

72
00:04:11,241 --> 00:04:12,240
and make you want to move.

73
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,240
The ADS represents just the latest effort

74
00:04:17,241 --> 00:04:22,040
the latest effort to devise an effective ray weapon.

75
00:04:22,041 --> 00:04:26,720
Prior to and during World War II, both the Allies and Axis powers

76
00:04:26,721 --> 00:04:29,340
were unsuccessful in their attempts to craft a death ray

77
00:04:29,341 --> 00:04:31,280
using radio waves and microwaves.

78
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,400
But their research accidentally led to the development of radar.

79
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,880
In the 1960s, scientists pioneered the first working lasers.

80
00:04:43,881 --> 00:04:46,320
Essentially, a laser generates the particles

81
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:50,860
of light called photons and focuses them into a precise beam.

82
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:56,320
But a powerful laser weapon was clearly still a long way off.

83
00:04:56,321 --> 00:05:00,100
By the 1970s at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico,

84
00:05:00,101 --> 00:05:03,400
a laser was showing true potential as a weapon,

85
00:05:03,401 --> 00:05:06,100
the electric discharge coaxial laser.

86
00:05:06,101 --> 00:05:08,660
It's still in use there today.

87
00:05:08,661 --> 00:05:11,420
It is a gas laser, which runs off of nitrogen and carbon

88
00:05:11,421 --> 00:05:13,720
dioxide, and we're ready to roll.

89
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:20,320
So right now, you can see the effects of the chemicals

90
00:05:20,321 --> 00:05:22,320
as they're getting excited.

91
00:05:22,321 --> 00:05:25,320
Basically, we have 40,000 volts that are traveling

92
00:05:25,321 --> 00:05:29,320
through this laser that's causing the excitation of nitrogen

93
00:05:29,321 --> 00:05:31,320
gas, which is blowing that pinkish-purple color.

94
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:34,320
That, in turn, caused the chain reaction to excite the carbon

95
00:05:34,321 --> 00:05:35,320
dioxide.

96
00:05:35,321 --> 00:05:37,320
It emits photons, which is creating the laser.

97
00:05:37,321 --> 00:05:41,320
And right behind me is the target plexiglass.

98
00:05:41,321 --> 00:05:44,320
It's burning right now.

99
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:48,720
The next demonstration targets the very hard metal titanium.

100
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:52,120
The invisible beam is bounced off four mirrors

101
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:54,840
to make sure the laser equipment is a safe distance

102
00:05:54,841 --> 00:05:56,520
from the sparks that are about to fly.

103
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:10,860
Over the decades of testing that we've performed over in this lab,

104
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:13,940
there's pretty much no material that is impervious

105
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:14,920
to laser damage.

106
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:20,520
By 1983, spectacular demonstrations like this

107
00:06:20,521 --> 00:06:22,520
helped encourage President Ronald Reagan

108
00:06:22,521 --> 00:06:25,220
to propose the construction of strange weapons

109
00:06:25,221 --> 00:06:27,840
under the banner, the Strategic Defense Initiative,

110
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:30,000
quickly nicknamed Star Wars.

111
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:32,760
Part of the early focus of the project

112
00:06:32,761 --> 00:06:34,740
was the concept of deploying satellites

113
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:37,480
containing X-ray lasers that, when activated,

114
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,640
would create an impenetrable barrier

115
00:06:39,641 --> 00:06:41,100
to incoming enemy missiles.

116
00:06:41,101 --> 00:06:44,820
At the time, it sent chills behind the Iron Curtain.

117
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:49,600
However, the plan was far too ambitious, both scientifically and financially,

118
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:52,420
and Star Wars remained a fantasy.

119
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:55,480
Still, research continued.

120
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:01,440
And two decades later, lasers are finally entering the battlefield.

121
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:06,840
The Boeing company redesigned its Avenger Humvee system

122
00:07:06,841 --> 00:07:10,940
to include a one-kilowatt solid-state laser to its onboard arsenal.

123
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:15,100
It was designed not to target enemy soldiers,

124
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:17,100
but improvised explosive devices

125
00:07:17,101 --> 00:07:21,100
and unexploded ordnance that threatened U.S. troops.

126
00:07:21,101 --> 00:07:25,100
The laser is located here in the turret,

127
00:07:25,101 --> 00:07:27,100
and then it feeds by an optical fiber

128
00:07:27,101 --> 00:07:29,100
up to the beam director,

129
00:07:29,101 --> 00:07:31,100
where the light is expanded and stabilized.

130
00:07:31,101 --> 00:07:34,100
Then it's emitted from the front of this aperture here,

131
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:36,100
out to the target.

132
00:07:36,101 --> 00:07:40,100
Unlike a normal light, a laser beam is coherent,

133
00:07:40,101 --> 00:07:42,980
is coherent, directional, and extremely intense.

134
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,820
This is the gunner's console.

135
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:50,320
This is where we control all of the firing mechanisms of the vehicle,

136
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:51,900
as well as turret control.

137
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,540
To move the turret, you pull back on the yoke,

138
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:59,040
and left, right moves that,

139
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:01,420
and then you have up-down, your elevation axis.

140
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:06,200
When you acquire a target,

141
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:09,040
we will change the field of view to zoom in on the target,

142
00:08:09,041 --> 00:08:12,260
and then you engage the power settings

143
00:08:12,261 --> 00:08:15,520
and pulling back on the trigger, engage the laser.

144
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:17,960
Basically, we align the beam

145
00:08:17,961 --> 00:08:21,300
so that a small spot size appears onto the munition

146
00:08:21,301 --> 00:08:24,580
and then it causes the outer casing of the munition to heat

147
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:26,660
and then that heat flows inside

148
00:08:26,661 --> 00:08:28,700
and causes the high explosive to detonate.

149
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:31,800
As lasers like the Avengers

150
00:08:31,801 --> 00:08:34,560
are becoming valuable defensive weapons on the ground,

151
00:08:34,561 --> 00:08:39,940
Airborne lasers are being ready to blast enemy missiles out of the sky.

152
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:46,480
The airborne laser is an offshoot of the Star Wars program.

153
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:51,440
What we've done is we've brought the technology down to a level that we can manage.

154
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:56,600
Instead of putting satellites into orbit, we're putting that technology onto aircraft.

155
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:03,120
A decade from now, strategists hope to deploy a fleet of laser-equipped 747s

156
00:09:03,121 --> 00:09:05,620
that can detect enemy missiles as they're launched

157
00:09:05,621 --> 00:09:08,220
and destroy them from several hundred miles away.

158
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:12,000
The airborne laser, as a platform,

159
00:09:12,001 --> 00:09:14,920
will fly a predefined orbit,

160
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:17,560
and we look at that as a figure-eight pattern

161
00:09:17,561 --> 00:09:22,160
flying off the border of the country

162
00:09:22,161 --> 00:09:23,280
that we're concerned about.

163
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:26,160
The distinctive external feature

164
00:09:26,161 --> 00:09:27,700
of the airborne laser aircraft

165
00:09:27,701 --> 00:09:30,000
is the large turret mounted on its nose.

166
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:33,340
This is the real weapon part of the aircraft.

167
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:36,840
The front of the turret is a conformal window,

168
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:41,120
weighs about 288 pounds, formed out of a single piece of glass.

169
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:43,660
The turret's able to rotate in two directions.

170
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:47,280
You have the rotation of the shell and the rotation of the ball.

171
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:52,540
This allows us to point at anywhere in space as the aircraft flies.

172
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:56,780
This defensive weapon system consists of three different lasers.

173
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:59,820
After sensors detect a missile launch,

174
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:03,040
the first laser fires what is essentially a tracking beam

175
00:10:03,041 --> 00:10:04,580
to provide targeting data.

176
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:08,560
Then the second laser measures atmospheric distortions.

177
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:11,240
Finally, a third laser fires,

178
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:14,660
a destructive beam that compensates for those distortions

179
00:10:14,661 --> 00:10:15,980
and strikes its target.

180
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,260
This strange weapon's ability to compensate

181
00:10:20,261 --> 00:10:22,540
for atmospheric distortions was developed here,

182
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:26,360
at the Air Force's Starfire Optical Range in New Mexico.

183
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:31,100
It corrects images captured by the facility's telescope.

184
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:35,000
The atmosphere causes light to get distorted,

185
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:37,620
just like looking down a hot road on a summer day.

186
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:40,960
You see that heat coming up and it changes the image you see.

187
00:10:42,580 --> 00:10:47,080
Such distortions are countered by the computerized adaptive optic system.

188
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:49,940
The key component is a pliable mirror

189
00:10:49,941 --> 00:10:53,420
that's continually reshaped to cancel out the atmospheric clutter.

190
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:59,580
Inside the airborne laser, an adaptive optics mirror distorts the destructive beam as it's fired.

191
00:11:00,620 --> 00:11:08,260
While the beam travels through the sky, the atmospheric distortions reshape it back into a precise beam that can destroy its target.

192
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:14,200
In real time, all three lasers will engage and destroy the target within just a couple of seconds.

193
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:17,840
Then, if need be, they will re-aim to destroy additional missiles.

194
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:22,940
The destructive third laser is a chemical oxygen iodine laser.

195
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:25,460
or coil.

196
00:11:25,461 --> 00:11:28,400
Generating its powerful beam requires a bulky assortment

197
00:11:28,401 --> 00:11:30,900
of equipment and chemical vats that takes up most

198
00:11:30,901 --> 00:11:34,120
of the 747's cargo space.

199
00:11:34,121 --> 00:11:37,060
We're putting out in the megawatt class of energy,

200
00:11:37,061 --> 00:11:40,000
the types of power needed to destroy a missile

201
00:11:40,001 --> 00:11:42,140
at those military ranges that we're looking at,

202
00:11:42,141 --> 00:11:45,500
several hundred kilometers.

203
00:11:45,501 --> 00:11:47,900
In a more earthbound pursuit, researchers

204
00:11:47,901 --> 00:11:51,160
at California's Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory

205
00:11:51,161 --> 00:11:55,960
are working on developing a less cumbersome and safer laser weapon.

206
00:11:55,961 --> 00:11:59,540
Their lasers are powered not by chemicals but by electricity,

207
00:11:59,541 --> 00:12:04,340
using the same basic tech as a common laser pointer.

208
00:12:04,341 --> 00:12:07,460
What I'm holding in my hand is actually a diode-pumped,

209
00:12:07,461 --> 00:12:10,960
neodymium-ion-based solid-state laser.

210
00:12:10,961 --> 00:12:13,500
And you can see that using this laser pointer,

211
00:12:13,501 --> 00:12:16,740
you can make a small spot at a reasonable distance.

212
00:12:16,741 --> 00:12:19,540
And that's because this is a very good beam quality system.

213
00:12:19,541 --> 00:12:22,540
This laser is a one milliwatt average power laser.

214
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:23,800
It's a thousandth of a watt.

215
00:12:23,801 --> 00:12:29,800
The laser you're going to see is 10 million to 100 million times higher average power

216
00:12:29,801 --> 00:12:32,120
than this particular laser I'm holding in my hand.

217
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:37,220
What you see here is the world's most powerful diode pump solid state laser

218
00:12:37,221 --> 00:12:39,740
called the solid state heat capacity laser.

219
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:44,040
We have achieved 67 kilowatts of average output power

220
00:12:44,041 --> 00:12:47,720
and a footprint roughly 6 foot by 8 foot in size.

221
00:12:47,721 --> 00:12:55,100
With eventual portability in mind, the laser runs on batteries, a bank of lithium-ion batteries.

222
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:02,620
The test target for this demonstration is an aluminum sheet, and it will be hit by a beam four inches square.

223
00:13:02,621 --> 00:13:16,621
It has taken years to deliver a weapons-quality beam and equipment this small.

224
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:26,300
So for now, it looks like handheld laser guns will remain science fiction in the foreseeable future.

225
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:30,460
But there are plenty of other portable devices already out there,

226
00:13:30,461 --> 00:13:35,980
able to blast our eyes and ears and turn a walk into a wipeout.

227
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:45,800
Scientists have been trying to invent a death ray composed of x-rays or gamma rays for years,

228
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:47,940
but nature beat them to it.

229
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:55,420
In 2007, NASA discovered a black hole generating such a being that's destroying everything in its path.

230
00:13:56,020 --> 00:13:57,880
It's been dubbed the Death Star.

231
00:13:57,881 --> 00:14:01,660
Strange Weapons will return on Modern Marvels.

232
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:09,480
Law enforcement agencies and the military are always looking for the latest in less than lethal technologies.

233
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,735
One strange weapon developed for the U.S.

234
00:14:14,736 --> 00:14:18,420
military by the Southwest Research Institute in San Antonio, Texas,

235
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:20,640
will literally knock you off your feet.

236
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:26,760
Scientists there call this anti-traction material the mobility denial system.

237
00:14:26,761 --> 00:14:30,560
It can turn just about any surface into a slip and slide.

238
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:36,480
It's designed to trip up aggressors trying to enter restricted areas like embassies and military bases.

239
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:45,420
The anti-traction material provides the military with a way of providing a standoff distance between them and a riotous crowd.

240
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:51,200
This is the anti-traction material powder in a dry form.

241
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:52,480
It's an acrylamide polymer.

242
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:55,920
Similar to in consistency to salt.

243
00:14:55,921 --> 00:14:58,560
It's very fine.

244
00:14:58,561 --> 00:15:03,520
We'll mix a little bit up here, pour a little water in.

245
00:15:03,521 --> 00:15:07,660
You can see it immediately starts to absorb the water.

246
00:15:07,661 --> 00:15:12,580
It becomes this gelatinous mass that is very, very slimy

247
00:15:12,581 --> 00:15:14,480
and slippery.

248
00:15:14,481 --> 00:15:16,780
We've set up this demonstration to show you the effects of

249
00:15:16,781 --> 00:15:20,220
friction and how the anti-traction material negates that.

250
00:15:20,221 --> 00:15:22,820
This steel block weighs about 20 pounds.

251
00:15:22,821 --> 00:15:25,920
Up here on the dry part of the wood, it doesn't move.

252
00:15:25,921 --> 00:15:28,560
From here down, we've coated it with anti-attraction material.

253
00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:32,440
So as you can see, as I push it onto that, it will slide all the way down.

254
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:37,940
To assess the effectiveness of this slippery goo,

255
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:41,040
technicians have sprayed it inside this tented area

256
00:15:41,041 --> 00:15:45,240
