00:30:49,760 --> 00:30:52,200
Strange Weapons will return on Modern Marvel.

492
00:30:53,900 --> 00:30:56,640
Today, there's a variety of unusual robots

493
00:30:56,641 --> 00:31:00,400
available to deal with dangerous military and law enforcement situations.

494
00:31:03,480 --> 00:31:06,320
If you're coping with a cornered enemy or a hostage crisis,

495
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:11,440
the small but versatile recon scout will tread into the lion's den for the first look.

496
00:31:13,200 --> 00:31:17,280
The main advantage of having a robot such as this is instead of actually putting your team

497
00:31:17,281 --> 00:31:20,200
members at risk, you can send a robot like this.

498
00:31:20,201 --> 00:31:23,120
This one in particular can be thrown in and will

499
00:31:23,121 --> 00:31:27,200
self-orient, and that'll function as that extra set of eyes

500
00:31:27,201 --> 00:31:29,440
to find a culprit hiding in an environment.

501
00:31:30,480 --> 00:31:33,040
The recon scout has two antennae.

502
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:36,240
One receives the signals from the remote control unit

503
00:31:36,241 --> 00:31:37,840
that's operated by a single toggle.

504
00:31:38,800 --> 00:31:41,900
The other sends the camera's video signal back to the operator.

505
00:31:42,860 --> 00:31:45,040
The weighted tail on the back of the robot

506
00:31:45,041 --> 00:31:46,800
keeps its camera properly oriented.

507
00:31:48,580 --> 00:31:51,740
This strange mechanical warrior, the Robo-Lobster,

508
00:31:51,741 --> 00:31:53,120
is still in development.

509
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:57,200
It's designed to search out and detonate mines on the ocean floor,

510
00:31:57,940 --> 00:32:02,040
including those placed in shallow water near beaches, targeting landing craft.

511
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:05,120
Built to behave like its crustacean namesake,

512
00:32:05,500 --> 00:32:09,560
it will have hardware to enable it to react to its surroundings with animal-like instinct.

513
00:32:10,180 --> 00:32:14,200
Once the robo-lobster is actually able to find a particular device,

514
00:32:14,460 --> 00:32:16,720
it itself is a suicide robot.

515
00:32:17,020 --> 00:32:20,680
So the idea is that you would send this robot in to blow up a device.

516
00:32:21,740 --> 00:32:25,480
You don't lose your explosives person.

517
00:32:25,980 --> 00:32:27,940
You're sacrificing the device.

518
00:32:29,320 --> 00:32:32,720
Other robots are designed to be aggressive warriors.

519
00:32:33,460 --> 00:32:37,300
The sword robot has a sturdy, low-profile tank-like body

520
00:32:37,301 --> 00:32:39,880
that carries a fully automatic machine gun.

521
00:32:41,260 --> 00:32:45,560
The tank treads allow it to traverse very uneven and unstable ground.

522
00:32:47,560 --> 00:32:50,840
The operator with the remote can stay a safe distance from the action.

523
00:32:51,740 --> 00:32:56,120
while putting those it confronts at a distinct psychological disadvantage.

524
00:32:56,980 --> 00:33:01,100
One wrong move against this Terminator, and it's hasta la vista, baby.

525
00:33:01,740 --> 00:33:06,800
They know they don't have the advantage of being able to get in a confrontation with a person.

526
00:33:06,940 --> 00:33:09,960
They're facing a robot. If they shoot the robot, the robot doesn't care.

527
00:33:10,380 --> 00:33:11,440
It may shoot back, too.

528
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:17,780
Robots represent the epitome of how today's strange weapons diminish the human element.

529
00:33:17,781 --> 00:33:24,200
But odd weaponry of the past relied heavily on the skill of those who wielded it.

530
00:33:24,840 --> 00:33:28,000
Centuries ago, conflicts almost always ended up hand to hand.

531
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:32,380
And none were better armed and more adept at this than the ninjas,

532
00:33:33,040 --> 00:33:37,280
Japan's stealth resistance fighters that opposed the establishment in feudal Japan.

533
00:33:37,800 --> 00:33:40,200
The weapon in my hand is called the Kyoketsu Shoge.

534
00:33:40,360 --> 00:33:43,120
It's actually a combination weapon, and it uses a ring,

535
00:33:43,121 --> 00:33:47,120
which can actually be whirled and swung and uses a striking implement.

536
00:33:47,780 --> 00:33:48,920
through the use of the chain.

537
00:33:48,921 --> 00:33:51,080
The chain can actually be used to entangle

538
00:33:51,081 --> 00:33:54,020
and ensnare the opponent.

539
00:33:54,021 --> 00:33:57,020
Now I use the chain up around the head.

540
00:33:57,021 --> 00:34:00,820
From in here, you can use the dagger.

541
00:34:00,821 --> 00:34:03,100
In feudal Japan, only the samurai

542
00:34:03,101 --> 00:34:05,000
were allowed to carry weapons.

543
00:34:05,001 --> 00:34:07,800
To avoid arrest, ninjas invented many ways

544
00:34:07,801 --> 00:34:10,460
to hide their weapons in plain sight.

545
00:34:10,461 --> 00:34:12,780
A robed sash with weights added to both ends

546
00:34:12,781 --> 00:34:15,240
could be a very effective weapon.

547
00:34:15,241 --> 00:34:17,600
The jute was originally a farming tool.

548
00:34:17,601 --> 00:34:22,340
used by the Japanese farmer for produce and to remove weeds.

549
00:34:22,341 --> 00:34:24,580
It was used by the ninja and incorporated

550
00:34:24,581 --> 00:34:26,580
into their vast arsenal of weapons

551
00:34:26,581 --> 00:34:30,220
as a very effective tool against the samurai katana.

552
00:34:30,221 --> 00:34:32,380
The hook was used to catch the katana.

553
00:34:34,820 --> 00:34:38,720
The beauty of ninjutsu was that the katana of the samurai

554
00:34:38,721 --> 00:34:40,960
was used against him and he cut his own throat

555
00:34:40,961 --> 00:34:42,120
as he fell to the ground.

556
00:34:42,121 --> 00:34:47,120
Ninjas strap these claws, called shuko, to their hands.

557
00:34:47,220 --> 00:34:50,120
They use them to help climb trees or walls,

558
00:34:50,220 --> 00:34:53,660
and in combat, to block sword attacks and rip flesh.

559
00:34:56,160 --> 00:34:59,580
These shuko, in darkness, faded away,

560
00:34:59,680 --> 00:35:01,680
and it was thought the ninja caught the katana

561
00:35:01,780 --> 00:35:03,780
with their bare hands.

562
00:35:03,880 --> 00:35:08,780
These claws thus enhance the mystique and legend of the ninja.

563
00:35:08,781 --> 00:35:15,920
of the ninjas. Elsewhere in Asia, India had come up with a similar claw weapon.

564
00:35:17,240 --> 00:35:21,000
But this region of the world is more renowned for its exotic blades.

565
00:35:22,820 --> 00:35:28,170
This is a pata from North India. It dates to about the early part of the 1700s.

566
00:35:28,171 --> 00:35:28,871
And it is

567
00:35:28,872 --> 00:35:35,325
both a beautiful and a formidable weapon. It locks my hand onto the interior.

568
00:35:35,326 --> 00:35:36,026
My hand

569
00:35:36,027 --> 00:35:40,940
is wrapped around a crossbar and locked within this kind of gauntlet,

570
00:35:41,120 --> 00:35:45,720
which makes the double-edged blade really just an extension of my forearm.

571
00:35:45,860 --> 00:35:52,000
So every time I swing it, the entire force of my body is behind that swing.

572
00:35:53,620 --> 00:35:56,260
This is a jandahar, also from India.

573
00:35:56,840 --> 00:36:00,080
It is normally found as a single blade to pierce chainmail,

574
00:36:00,700 --> 00:36:02,420
worn by most Indian potentates.

575
00:36:02,740 --> 00:36:03,860
This one is a bit unique.

576
00:36:03,861 --> 00:36:08,120
When you squeeze these two bars right here, the blade opens up.

577
00:36:08,360 --> 00:36:12,980
Thought to be an armor piercer, it is a beautiful piece in Gold Kofkari,

578
00:36:13,380 --> 00:36:16,460
but certainly something less than utilitarian.

579
00:36:17,420 --> 00:36:22,280
On the other hand, this piece is a deadly fighting knife called a holiday,

580
00:36:23,200 --> 00:36:24,660
and it is black steel.

581
00:36:25,120 --> 00:36:27,000
It also has armor-piercing tips.

582
00:36:27,400 --> 00:36:32,020
That is, the tips are thickened, and it is one ferocious fighting weapon.

583
00:36:33,860 --> 00:36:36,900
European smiths, renowned for their superior plate armor,

584
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:40,400
also crafted some small but specialized shields.

585
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:42,960
This is an example of a buckler,

586
00:36:43,240 --> 00:36:45,580
but it's got a couple of extra features.

587
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:49,860
This door here conceals a small candle behind

588
00:36:49,861 --> 00:36:53,200
that you would use perhaps to light your way a little bit,

589
00:36:53,260 --> 00:36:54,780
but more important, in a fight,

590
00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:56,220
it provides a little bit of light

591
00:36:56,221 --> 00:36:57,840
that will distract your opponent

592
00:36:57,841 --> 00:37:01,060
as you're defending yourself in the dead of night

593
00:37:01,061 --> 00:37:02,220
against some assailant.

594
00:37:02,221 --> 00:37:04,800
and once you have distracted them suitably,

595
00:37:04,980 --> 00:37:07,180
you can come in perhaps with a cut for the sword

596
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:09,440
or with this point,

597
00:37:10,820 --> 00:37:13,040
a punch to the face for the final blow.

598
00:37:14,000 --> 00:37:15,440
While this may have been enough

599
00:37:15,441 --> 00:37:17,400
to dazzle opponents in Shakespeare's day,

600
00:37:18,680 --> 00:37:20,840
imagine their reaction to the detonation

601
00:37:20,841 --> 00:37:22,600
of the father of all bombs.

602
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:29,260
Pentagon documents from 1994

603
00:37:29,261 --> 00:37:31,100
indicate the U.S. military

604
00:37:31,101 --> 00:37:33,440
had investigated building a gay bomb.

605
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:36,400
It would have contained strong aphrodisiacs

606
00:37:36,401 --> 00:37:39,740
to encourage the enemy to make love, not war.

607
00:37:40,680 --> 00:37:43,120
Strange Weapons will return on Modern Marvels.

608
00:37:47,220 --> 00:37:50,260
Researchers are always experimenting with new technologies

609
00:37:50,261 --> 00:37:52,780
to improve the performance of established weaponry.

610
00:37:56,700 --> 00:38:00,200
Even the tried-and-true flashbang is getting a high-tech makeover.

611
00:38:01,100 --> 00:38:03,760
at Sandia National Laboratories.

612
00:38:04,740 --> 00:38:07,480
This is the latest flashbang technology.

613
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:12,680
Conventional flashbang, the body of the device explodes

614
00:38:12,681 --> 00:38:14,780
when the explosive inside the device functions.

615
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:19,120
In our design, there is no explosive inside the flashbang body.

616
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:22,740
Here's a cutaway of our flashbang.

617
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:25,060
What we have is a standard fuse assembly.

618
00:38:25,061 --> 00:38:27,340
When you pull the pin, the fuse is ignited.

619
00:38:27,480 --> 00:38:30,400
That lights a small gas generator charge.

620
00:38:30,401 --> 00:38:32,500
that pressurizes the body of the device.

621
00:38:32,780 --> 00:38:34,600
This area is filled with aluminum powder.

622
00:38:35,080 --> 00:38:38,100
That aluminum powder is ejected out of these radial holes.

623
00:38:40,560 --> 00:38:43,080
That aluminum burns rapidly with the atmosphere

624
00:38:43,081 --> 00:38:45,420
and gives you a bright flash.

625
00:38:45,760 --> 00:38:48,160
The sound is produced by the rapid expansion of air.

626
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:54,420
This new type of flashbang is essentially a thermobaric device.

627
00:38:55,800 --> 00:38:59,840
Take this same principle and multiply the size by thousands of times.

628
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:04,000
And you have one of today's most horrific weapons,

629
00:39:04,100 --> 00:39:05,400
the thermobaric bomb.

630
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:15,140
Its name is derived from the Greek words for heat and pressure.

631
00:39:15,240 --> 00:39:18,260
Thermobarics use an explosion to disperse and ignite

632
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:22,260
a cloud of metallic powder, such as aluminum.

633
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:25,600
The airborne burning fuel creates a slower and more sustained

634
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:28,700
shockwave than a conventional bomb,

635
00:39:28,701 --> 00:39:32,600
perfect for destroying virtually inaccessible targets.

636
00:39:32,700 --> 00:39:36,000
You can think of an explosive device as maybe taking your fist

637
00:39:36,100 --> 00:39:38,280
and punching a wall, where you might punch a hole in it.

638
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:41,220
And a thermobaric device is more along the lines

639
00:39:41,320 --> 00:39:44,280
of standing close to the wall, putting both hands on it,

640
00:39:44,380 --> 00:39:46,080
and pushing on it real hard.

641
00:39:46,180 --> 00:39:48,460
And that's why it's more effective at knocking down

642
00:39:48,560 --> 00:39:51,120
walls and destroying caves.

643
00:39:51,220 --> 00:39:53,500
If the explosive power of this strange weapon

644
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:57,960
doesn't kill you, another of its properties will.

645
00:39:57,961 --> 00:40:02,120
If you were in a cave and a thermobaric device were deployed,

646
00:40:02,720 --> 00:40:06,620
there would be a fireball that would come down the cave,

647
00:40:06,720 --> 00:40:10,100
and if that missed you, if you were too deep for that,

648
00:40:10,101 --> 00:40:14,580
the fact that all the oxygen were used up would mean that you'd suffocate.

649
00:40:15,680 --> 00:40:19,000
Currently, the United States finds itself pitted against Russia

650
00:40:19,001 --> 00:40:21,300
in a thermobaric arms race of sorts.

651
00:40:22,860 --> 00:40:27,420
In September 2007, the Russians dropped a huge thermobaric device.

652
00:40:27,960 --> 00:40:34,320
The massive fireball and pressure wave demolished an abandoned apartment complex.

653
00:40:34,800 --> 00:40:36,920
They proclaimed it the father of all bombs.

654
00:40:37,520 --> 00:40:38,220
But is it?

655
00:40:38,620 --> 00:40:42,340
If you pose that against something like an atomic bomb,

656
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:47,760
an atomic bomb is still several orders of magnitude more energetic than thermal barracks.

657
00:40:48,720 --> 00:40:52,140
The atom bombs dropped on Japan at the end of World War II,

658
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:56,260
with the devastating climax of a war that saw some incredible innovation

659
00:40:56,261 --> 00:40:58,160
and aerial bombardment technologies.

660
00:41:04,900 --> 00:41:08,180
England produced what is perhaps the strangest bomb of the war,

661
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:09,660
the dam buster.

662
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:13,980
It came about when Britain encountered problems with its plan

663
00:41:13,981 --> 00:41:17,280
to destroy key dams supplying power to the German war machine.

664
00:41:18,740 --> 00:41:22,660
Dropping a bomb on a very narrow dam is nearly impossible to do.

665
00:41:22,661 --> 00:41:25,700
The bomb has to come right down and hit right on the edge of the dam

666
00:41:25,701 --> 00:41:26,960
or it will do no good.

667
00:41:27,640 --> 00:41:30,340
The other way of destroying a dam is to fire a torpedo

668
00:41:30,341 --> 00:41:32,460
through the water into the side of the dam,

669
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:35,620
but Germany had erected torpedo nets, making that impossible.

670
00:41:36,740 --> 00:41:39,020
British aircraft designer Barnes Wallace

671
00:41:39,021 --> 00:41:40,900
came up with the unconventional solution.

672
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:44,520
Wallace thought up this great idea

673
00:41:44,521 --> 00:41:48,200
where if we package the bomb again in a unique delivery system,

674
00:41:48,540 --> 00:41:52,580
we could drop it out of a plane and have it skip across the water,

675
00:41:52,581 --> 00:41:55,320
not sink into the water, but skip across the water

676
00:41:55,321 --> 00:41:59,040
until it hit the dam structure itself and then detonate.

677
00:42:00,040 --> 00:42:04,040
Wallace's final design was a cylindrical bomb 50 inches in diameter,

678
00:42:04,540 --> 00:42:09,360
60 inches long, and filled with 6,600 pounds of powerful explosive.

679
00:42:11,380 --> 00:42:14,520
For the mission, it was determined that each Lancaster bomber

680
00:42:14,521 --> 00:42:17,560
needed to be flying at 220 miles an hour,

681
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:19,700
just 60 feet above the water,

682
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:24,060
and 425 yards from the dam at the time the bomb was dropped.

683
00:42:24,980 --> 00:42:32,360
Prior to its release, the bomb mounted on pivots was spun at over 500 RPM by a small motor built into the bomb bay.

684
00:42:34,200 --> 00:42:39,700
It was incredibly important that the dam buster bomb rotate backwards.

685
00:42:39,900 --> 00:42:41,880
It had to be designed to do that.

686
00:42:42,380 --> 00:42:50,300
If it were to rotate forward, then as it struck the dam, it would simply go up and over the dam, over on the other side and blow up harmlessly.

687
00:42:50,301 --> 00:42:54,840
But by rotating backward, when it struck the dam

688
00:42:54,841 --> 00:42:56,820
after it was bouncing across the water,

689
00:42:56,960 --> 00:43:00,700
it would actually go down into the water and then blow up.

690
00:43:01,920 --> 00:43:05,360
During the night raid on March 16, 1943,

691
00:43:05,860 --> 00:43:08,800
Wallace's dam buster successfully breached two dams

692
00:43:08,801 --> 00:43:11,360
and damaged a third.

693
00:43:13,540 --> 00:43:17,000
One of today's strangest aerial bombardment technologies

694
00:43:17,001 --> 00:43:18,260
is now under development.

695
00:43:18,261 --> 00:43:21,100
It's the US Navy's delivery system for thousands

696
00:43:21,200 --> 00:43:24,160
of so-called venom darts.

697
00:43:24,260 --> 00:43:27,160
The darts are about a foot long, and the venom they release

698
00:43:27,260 --> 00:43:30,700
on impact is actually a highly corrosive chemical called

699
00:43:30,800 --> 00:43:34,680
diethylene triamine, or DITA.

700
00:43:34,780 --> 00:43:36,740
Luckily for enemy combatants, this

701
00:43:36,840 --> 00:43:38,840
is not being designed as an offensive weapon

702
00:43:38,940 --> 00:43:42,380
to be used against them, but rather as a way

703
00:43:42,480 --> 00:43:44,020
to clear minefields.

704
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:46,780
The darts are effective in up to 12 feet of water

705
00:43:46,781 --> 00:43:50,620
of water and two feet of sand.

706
00:43:50,621 --> 00:43:52,780
OK, good impact.

707
00:43:52,781 --> 00:43:55,080
Here, a venom dart is shot into a test tank

708
00:43:55,081 --> 00:43:57,360
to show how a mine, if it's not detonated

709
00:43:57,361 --> 00:43:59,400
by the initial impact of the dart,

710
00:43:59,401 --> 00:44:03,200
can be overpressurized by the release of the Dita.

711
00:44:03,201 --> 00:44:06,260
The chemical reaction has begun, and this show is about

712
00:44:06,261 --> 00:44:08,240
to go out with a bang.

713
00:44:08,241 --> 00:44:12,240
In the few seconds we have left, let's wonder, or worry,

714
00:44:12,241 --> 00:44:16,480
about the strange weapons that await us in the future.

